findings.description:
"Persistence accepts a PR target" says only that a PR review "saves under a PR-keyed name that satisfies the review frontmatter contract without a slice index." The name is not decoration: it is the only durable key for the artifact, and at least four existing consumers glob the reviews directory by *index*:
- `metrology/capture.py:resolve_target` globs `{index}-review.*` and errors on ambiguity;
- `metrology/discovery.py:discover_judge_results` globs `*-review.*` in `REVIEWS_SUBDIR`;
- `review/resolution_evidence.py:locate_review` globs `{index}-review.{type}.*.md` and `review_type_of()` splits the filename at position 1;
- `review/resolution_artifact.py:next_revision` globs a `{index}-review.{type}.{slice}...` pattern.

Because a PR number and a slice index are both small integers, any PR name beginning with a bare number makes `sq review resolve 380` or `sq metrology capture 380` silently able to bind to the wrong artifact. The name also determines whether re-reviewing a PR overwrites-and-archives (one artifact) or accumulates (head sha in the name) — which is exactly the difference between "Archiving ... apply unchanged" being true or false. The document must at least state the constraint the name has to satisfy (not index-shaped, parseable by `review_type_of`) and who reads it. `Anticipated Slices` lists "the PR naming and frontmatter" as a task, but the constraint is architectural, not incidental.
